Sure, here are some examples of how you can use the `v-slot` directive in Vue.js templates:

1. Basic usage:
```
<template>
  <div v-slot="slotProps">
    {{ slotProps }}
  </div>
</template>
```
In this example, we define a template with a single `<div>` element that has a `v-slot` attribute set to `"slotProps"`. The value of the `v-slot` attribute is used as the name of the variable that will be passed into the component instance. In this case, the variable is named "slotProps".

2. Using multiple slots:
```
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p>{{ description }}</p>
    <button @click="$emit('increment')">+</button>
    <button @click="$emit('decrement')">-</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  props: ['title', 'description'],
  data() {
    return {
      count: 0
    }
  },
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.count++
    },
    decrement() {
      this.count--
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we define a template with two buttons that emit events when clicked. The `v-slot` directive is not used directly on these elements, but rather on their parent container (the `<div>` element). This allows us to pass the event handlers from the child components up to the parent component, where they can be handled by the appropriate method.

3. Nested slots:
```
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p>{{ description }}</p>
    <ul>
      <li v-for="item in items" :key="item.id">
        <span>{{ item.name }}</span>
        <button @click="$emit('removeItem', item)">Remove</button>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  props: ['items'],
  methods: {
    removeItem(item) {
      const index = this.items.indexOf(item);
      if (index !== -1) {
        this.items.splice(index, 1);
      }
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we define a nested list of items that can be removed by clicking the corresponding button. The `v-slot` directive is used on each `<li>` element to provide access to the current item being rendered. When an item is removed, the `removeItem()` method is called with the item object as its argument.

4. Dynamic slots:
```
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p>{{ description }}</p>
    <component :is="currentComponent"></component>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import ComponentA from './components/ComponentA';
import ComponentB from './components/ComponentB';

export default {
  data() {
    return {
      currentComponent: null
    }
  },
  mounted() {
    // Set the initial component based on some condition
    if (this.$route.params.type === 'a') {
      this.currentComponent = ComponentA;
    } else {
      this.currentComponent = ComponentB;
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we define a dynamic component that renders either `ComponentA` or `ComponentB` depending on the route parameters. The `v-slot` directive is not used directly on any elements, but rather on the root element (`<div>`) to indicate that the component should render inside the slot provided by the parent component.

These are just a few examples of how you can use the `v-slot` directive in Vue.js templates. By using slots effectively, you can make your components more flexible and reusable while also improving readability and maintainability of your codebase.
